I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the frozen PXRD workflow first, then build a phase inventory and refine it in GSAS-II. I’ll keep the refinement residual-led and verify the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project agree exactly.

The sample is MgO + WO₃ fired at 900 °C, so Mg–W–O products plus residual oxides are the chemically allowed inventory. The scan has 8,301 points over 9.90–100.09° 2θ; I’m now screening exact and analogue COD structures against the observed peak families before committing to a GSAS-II model.

The dominant family matches monoclinic wolframite MgWO₄ (COD 9013902): its 18.884, 23.835, 30.416/30.750, and 41.481° reflections correspond coherently to observed peaks at 19.238, 24.215, 30.810/31.115, and 41.862°. The nearly common positive displacement is physically consistent with specimen height plus a small cell mismatch. I’m fitting this phase first so that any secondary-phase evidence is judged from a clean residual rather than raw-peak coincidences.

The single-phase residual resolves into the characteristic room-temperature WO₃ triplet near 23.4–24.7°, with supporting families near 26.91, 29.23, 34.47, 42.18, and 50.21°. I’m comparing the physical monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ records under the identical background, shift, cell, and profile model; the first viable monoclinic record already lowers Rwp from 20.25% to about 11.13%, while alternate records with malformed atom typing or unstable refinement are being rejected record-by-record.

A third phase is supported independently by the 43.209° and 62.519° residuals, both tracking periclase MgO under the same specimen shift. Adding MgO lowers the best common-profile Rwp to 9.90% with positive scale; the current inventory is therefore MgWO₄ + room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ + residual MgO. I’ll now refine this three-phase inventory, challenge the near-equivalent WO₃ settings after coordinates/profile are coherent, and test size/strain/texture only where isolated families support them.

Refining only symmetry-allowed coordinates lowers Rwp from 9.90% to 8.81% (MgWO₄ gives the largest gain; WO₃ gives a smaller but reproducible gain). The refinements have reconverged without changing the three-phase inventory. I’m now using isolated low-, mid-, and high-angle families to separate common instrumental width from phase-specific size/strain before testing texture or roughness.

The width model now has replay-stable, non-bound terms: MgWO₄ isotropic size broadening (~0.18–0.24 µm depending on roughness treatment) and WO₃ uniaxial microstrain along b; size for WO₃ and all size/strain terms for MgO collapsed or hit limits and were rejected. Surface roughness is clearly supported as a low-angle intensity effect, but its two Suortti parameters are nearly collinear, so I reduced it to a one-parameter form before testing polarization and texture.

The polarization trial is taking longer than the earlier stages because all coordinate/profile terms are being replayed together; it is still running, not stalled. The accepted model remains saved independently, so this trial can be discarded safely if it does not finish cleanly.

Polarization is strongly supported rather than acting as a scale surrogate: it refined to about 0.59 with a small uncertainty and reduced Rwp from 8.34% to 8.10%, while the phase fractions and accepted width terms remained stable. I’m letting this coupled model reconverge fully before any texture comparison, since texture tests on a drifting polarization/roughness baseline would be misleading.

Texture is weak but reproducible only for [001] in MgWO₄ and WO₃ (ratios ≈0.986, supported across several 00l/non-00l families); alternate axes fit worse, and MgO texture trials were unstable, so MgO remains random. Releasing coordinates again under the accepted polarization/profile model gives a further real gain; WO₃ coordinates are still converging, so I’m finishing that structural step before revisiting the alternate WO₃ records.

A physical-screen check caught an important problem: the freely refined WO₃ oxygen coordinates created 1.59 Å O–O contacts, so that lower-Rwp coordinate branch is not acceptable. I’m reverting to the published physical WO₃ coordinates and will allow only modest bounded symmetry-preserving shifts; this also makes the alternate-record comparison decisive instead of letting a poor record deform into an unphysical surrogate.

The physical-record challenge now favors triclinic WO₃ (COD 2106293): with the identical nuisance model and unrefined published WO₃ coordinates it reaches Rwp 7.85%, versus 7.90% for monoclinic COD 2311041 and 8.47% for the older monoclinic record. After refining only MgWO₄ coordinates, the triclinic model reaches 7.81% while remaining physically sound. I’m testing only modest WO₃ coordinate relaxation now; if geometry degrades, the published triclinic coordinates will be retained.

The alternate-record test is complete: the older monoclinic WO₃ record is decisively worse, while triclinic COD 2106293 gives the best physical fit. Its tungsten coordinates refine by only ~0.01–0.03 Å; oxygen shifts are not reliable from this Cu X-ray pattern and are therefore held at the published values. I’m now comparing smooth analytic background bases so the final low-angle continuum is not represented by unnecessary polynomial wiggles.

The phase inventory is converging on MgWO₄ + residual WO₃ + MgO. I’m finishing the background comparison, then I’ll replay the selected project and export all deliverables directly from that exact model.

The independent GSAS-II replay is clean: all three phase scales are positive, the calculated profile is nonnegative, the background stays at 274–2360 counts, and no profile-validity flags were raised. The replay gives 76.77 wt% MgWO₄, 16.29 wt% WO₃, and 6.94 wt% MgO with Rwp 7.507%.

The final artifacts are now generated from the replayed `final.gpx`. I’m doing a last consistency pass on the CIF parsing, fraction normalization, and the exported profile so the submitted files agree numerically with the project.

Completed the three-phase crystalline model:

- MgWO₄, wolframite P2/c: 76.7677 wt%
- WO₃, triclinic P-1: 16.2874 wt%
- MgO, periclase Fm-3m: 6.9450 wt%

Independent GSAS-II replay gave Rwp = 7.50695% and GOF = 2.62797 with no physical-profile flags.

